{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0179ovP6a67TFH65ks7bFaE5","type":"tool_result","content":"  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T160000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"The PIT scope names an introduced path outside the copy as an unreadable source instead of a path that escapes the workspace\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: adapters\n    what_happened: >\n      `pitestScopeOf` (`src/adapters/stacks/maven/pitest-reader.ts`) reads each introduced Java source through\n      `WorkspaceFiles.text`, which throws `<path> escapes the workspace` for a path outside the copy. The scope\n      then notes `unreadable source <path>: <path> escapes the workspace; its classes were not mutated`, where\n      the scope used to note `<path> escapes the workspace and was not scoped`. Introduced by the branch e37s01,\n      which moved the containment of the read from the scope into the runner's `WorkspaceFiles`.\n    what_expected: >\n      The note of a refactored scope is the note it gave before: a path outside the copy is named as escaping\n      the workspace, not as an unreadable source.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Call `PITEST_ENGINE.scopeOf(workspaceFiles(ws), { '../Outside.java': [1] })` and read `notes`.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `WorkspaceFiles.text` reports a path outside the copy as an error, the same way it reports an absent or\n      unreadable file, and the reader cannot tell the two apart without a distinct error.\n    evidence: >\n      Read during the self-review of e37s01 against `mutationScopeOf` at 536c551 (`src/adapters/execution/mutation.ts`),\n      which checked the containment itself before reading. No test pins either note. The kernel never reaches the\n      case: introduced paths come from the candidate manifest, which is walked inside the copy.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Let `WorkspaceFiles` say that a path escapes the copy apart from a read error, and give the PIT scope its\n      former note, with a test on an escaping introduced path.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T170000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"A declared report path that climbs into a sibling directory whose name starts with the copy's is read as a report\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: adapters\n    what_happened: >\n      `readReports` (`src/adapters/execution/workspace-files.ts`) keeps a report path inside the copy by testing\n      `abs.startsWith(resolve(workspace))`, with no separator after the root. With the copy at `<d>/ws`, a\n      `report_path` of `../ws2/r.txt` resolves to `<d>/ws2/r.txt`, passes the test, and the file is read, handed to\n      the reader and kept in the object store. A reader that does not ask for `single_file` — the JUnit, LCOV,\n      JaCoCo, PIT, PMD, CPD, jest, ESLint and jscpd readers, and any reader a technology brings — reads it.\n    what_expected: >\n      A path outside the copy is never read: `readReports` tests the root followed by a separator, as\n      `readBoundedReport`, `introducedSources` and `readInside` do.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"With `<d>/ws` and `<d>/ws2/r.txt`, call `workspaceFiles('<d>/ws', '../ws2/r.txt').reports('text/plain')`: it returns `[{ name: '../ws2/r.txt', text: … }]`; with `{ single_file: true }` it returns `[]`.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The containment test of `readReports` omits the separator after the root that the other three reads of\n      `WorkspaceFiles` require.\n    evidence: >\n      Probed on 2026-10-07 on the branch e37s01, and read identical on main 536c551 (`runner.ts`, `readReports`).\n      The `report_path` comes from the control definition a technology declares, not from the project judged, so\n      the project cannot choose it.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Test the root followed by a separator in `readReports`, with a test on a sibling directory whose name starts\n      with the copy's.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T190000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"A writable path the project committed as a symbolic link grants the control's command a write outside the copy\"\n    severity: high\n    priority: p1\n    scope: adapters\n    what_happened: >\n      `profileFor` (`src/adapters/execution/runner.ts`) keeps a control's `writable_paths` that lie inside the copy\n      by their written path, then the Seatbelt profile (`src/adapters/sandbox/backends.ts`) grants\n      `(allow file-write* (subpath …))` on `real(p)`, which resolves the path through `realpathSync`. When the\n      project judged has committed that path as a link to a file or directory outside the copy, the grant is\n      written for the link's target, and the control's command writes there. A campaign of a control declaring\n      `writable_paths: [\"lines.txt\"]`, on a target whose `lines.txt` is a committed link to\n      `~/.495-campagnes/e37s01-r2-ecrit.txt`, overwrote that file with the command's report. The reads of the\n      runner refuse the link afterwards, but the write outside the copy has happened. The Maven and Node controls\n      declare `target`, the coverage directory and report files as writable: a project that commits `target` as\n      a link to a directory of the user's gets a write grant on that whole directory.\n    what_expected: >\n      A command of the project judged never writes outside the copy: a writable path whose real path leaves the\n      copy is not granted, and the control is refused or runs without it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"With `<d>/outside.txt` and a target that commits `lines.txt` as a link to it, run a control whose `writable_paths` is `[\\\"lines.txt\\\"]` and whose command writes `lines.txt` under the Seatbelt backend: `<d>/outside.txt` holds the command's output.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `profileFor` tests the containment of a writable path on its written string, and `real()` resolves it\n      through links the project judged wrote, so the sandbox grant follows them out of the copy. A plain write\n      outside the copy, or through a link to a path that was not granted, is denied by Seatbelt (probed with\n      `sandbox-exec` on 2026-10-07).\n    evidence: >\n      Observed on 2026-10-07 in the acceptance campaign `e37s01-r2-x1-ecriture-lien` at 1d7f4cc (Seatbelt backend,\n      evidence `facts.sandbox` = `seatbelt`); `profileFor` and `real()` read identical on main 536c551. The bubblewrap\n      backend binds each writable path with `--bind p p`, which follows the link the same way, unprobed.\n    risk_level: high\n    follow_ups: >\n      Grant a writable path only when its real path, or that of its deepest existing ancestor, lies inside the real\n      path of the copy, in both backends, with a test on a writable path committed as a link to a file and to a\n      directory outside the copy.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T210000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"The JDK version never enters the identity of the environment, so a change of JDK does not requalify a Maven control\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      `describeEnvironment` (`src/application/environment.ts`) records for each probed tool the first line of what\n      its command prints on stdout. `java -version` prints its version on stderr and nothing on stdout, so the\n      `java` tool of the identity is the empty string whenever a JDK is installed, whatever its version. Two\n      machines, or one machine before and after a JDK upgrade, share the same environment digest as far as Java\n      goes, and a qualification or a piece of evidence of a Maven control established under one JDK is reused\n      under the other.\n    what_expected: >\n      The identity of the environment carries the version line of the JDK the controls run, so a change of JDK is\n      a change of environment, as a change of `mvn` already is.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"`java -version 2>/dev/null | wc -c` prints 0 on a machine with a JDK; `describeEnvironment(..., { java: [\\\"java\\\", [\\\"-version\\\"]] }).facts.tools.java` is `\\\"\\\"`.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The probe reads stdout only. Before the tools were declared by each technology, the `java` probe had a branch\n      of its own that read stdout on success and the first line of stderr on failure, which gave the same empty\n      string on success.\n    evidence: >\n      Observed on 2026-10-07 on this machine (GraalVM CE 25.0.4.1): stdout of `java -version` is empty, stderr\n      carries `openjdk version \"25.0.4.1\" 2026-08-18`. The value was kept as it was while the probes moved to the\n      Maven technology, so that the identity of an existing environment does not change with them.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Let a probe read the first non-empty line of stdout, then of stderr, with a test on a probe that prints its\n      version on stderr; the environment digest of every machine with a JDK changes once.\n    status: open\n85:/** Whether a path or a directory pattern lies below a `node_modules` directory, at the project root or in a workspace package. */\n87:\treturn path.split(\"/\").includes(\"node_modules\");","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"375da00f-6e83-45b0-9570-3e41e578a826","uuid":"369495fd-a871-4027-bac0-2cd1c34a9dda","timestamp":"2026-10-07T18:43:26.963Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-07T160000\n    date: \"2026-10-07\"\n    title: \"The PIT scope names an introduced path outside the copy as an unreadable source instead of a path that escapes the workspace\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: adapters\n    what_happened: >\n      `pitestScopeOf` (`src/adapters/stacks/maven/pitest-reader.ts`) reads each introduced Java source through\n      `WorkspaceFiles.text`, which throws `<path> escapes the workspace` for a path outside the copy. The scope\n      then 